WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:08.260
My name is Rebecca
Hadi, and I'm here

00:00:08.260 --> 00:00:12.910
today to tell you about a
time that I crashed a database

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:17.470
and I learned some techniques to
help from ever doing that again

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:20.890
that I'm going to
share with you.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.830
I was working on the 31st
floor of an office building

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:27.190
in Downtown Seattle that had
a beautiful view of the Puget

00:00:27.190 --> 00:00:29.830
Sound and Olympic Mountains.

00:00:29.830 --> 00:00:32.229
I was working on the
marketing analytics team

00:00:32.229 --> 00:00:34.750
at a large fashion
retailer, and I

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:38.140
was working on a project that
involved migrating marketing,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.770
attribution, reporting from
our old clickstream vendor

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:42.975
to an in-house solution.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:48.530
This involved doing a backfill.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:53.340
Has anyone in the room
ever done a data backfill?

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:54.880
Yeah I see a few hands.

00:00:54.880 --> 00:00:59.490
Has anyone ever had performance
issues with that backfill?

00:00:59.490 --> 00:01:02.100
I certainly have.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.620
So this project required
a backfill of a year

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:08.190
and a half of historical
clickstream data.

00:01:08.190 --> 00:01:10.500
But at this time, I
had been a data analyst

00:01:10.500 --> 00:01:13.710
for about three years and
through that time had written

00:01:13.710 --> 00:01:16.470
SQL queries almost every day.

00:01:16.470 --> 00:01:20.010
So naturally, I thought
I was an expert.

00:01:20.010 --> 00:01:23.970
So I sat down at my desk,
put on my headphones,

00:01:23.970 --> 00:01:28.340
turned on a score by Trent
Reznor and got to work.

00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.420
I was working in an
Amazon Redshift database

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.300
with page view
level website data.

00:01:39.300 --> 00:01:42.090
And for those of you who may
not be familiar with website

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.290
data, any time that you hover
or click or view a page,

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:47.910
it's generating data.

00:01:47.910 --> 00:01:51.780
So you can imagine that that can
be a pretty large set of data

00:01:51.780 --> 00:01:54.930
for a single person, not to
mention a website that has

00:01:54.930 --> 00:01:57.490
millions of visitors a year.

00:01:57.490 --> 00:02:00.160
In the past, I had worked at
health insurance companies,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:03.550
and so I was used to working
with medical claims data,

00:02:03.550 --> 00:02:05.895
and that data was more
in the millions of rows.

00:02:08.419 --> 00:02:13.880
This data set was
4 billion rows.

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.260
So I had my query and I
sat I sat down at my desk

00:02:18.260 --> 00:02:21.630
and I had my two monitors.
On the right hand side,

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:25.310
I had my SQL IDE, and I
set off my backfill,

00:02:25.310 --> 00:02:28.520
and on my left monitor
I had some documentation

00:02:28.520 --> 00:02:30.320
open that I was working on.

00:02:30.320 --> 00:02:33.650
I updated some Jira tickets
and let's be honest,

00:02:33.650 --> 00:02:35.600
checked my Twitter feed.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:38.480
After getting sucked into
that for about 45 minutes,

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:41.570
I turned to my screen and I
noticed that my query was still

00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:43.280
running.

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:49.960
And I thought, hmm, that
doesn't seem very good.

00:02:49.960 --> 00:02:55.360
So I tried to cancel it, and I
got the rainbow wheel of death

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:58.410
and had to kill it.

00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:00.810
And then I tried
to run it again.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:03.650
And about an hour later,

00:03:03.650 --> 00:03:08.480
there was still a problem. So
I tried to cancel it again.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:10.620
And again, it crashed.

00:03:10.620 --> 00:03:13.950
So I thought to myself,
what is going on with this?

00:03:13.950 --> 00:03:16.770
I'm a SQL expert and
obviously my code is perfect,

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:20.460
so it can't be anything
that I'm doing.

00:03:20.460 --> 00:03:22.120
On the 31st floor,

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:24.450
it held the entire data
science and analytics

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:26.970
team, which was about
100 people made up

00:03:26.970 --> 00:03:30.150
of data analysts,
engineers and scientists.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:34.320
So I thought it's probably just
a busy time on the database.

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.810
I'm probably running into
some concurrency issues

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:39.710
and it's just slow in general.

00:03:39.710 --> 00:03:41.960
Then I had a brilliant idea.

00:03:41.960 --> 00:03:44.120
I'll just run it at night.

00:03:44.120 --> 00:03:46.040
Traffic on the
database will be low

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:48.650
and everything will be fine.

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:52.160
So I went home, took
the bus, and before I

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:53.810
went to bed that
night, I set off

00:03:53.810 --> 00:03:55.750
my query to run into the night.

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:01.400
I woke up a little bit
early the next morning

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:05.440
to a Slack notification
on my phone

00:04:05.440 --> 00:04:09.490
from the database administrator

00:04:09.490 --> 00:04:11.490
who had never direct
messaged me before.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:16.530
And all it said was, Hi.

00:04:20.440 --> 00:04:21.589
Oh, fudge.

00:04:21.589 --> 00:04:24.070
What have I done?

00:04:24.070 --> 00:04:27.160
My heart sank because I knew
that something was wrong.

00:04:27.160 --> 00:04:29.530
It turned out that
the query that I ran

00:04:29.530 --> 00:04:32.290
ran for over seven hours.

00:04:32.290 --> 00:04:34.690
And not only that, it
took compute resources

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:36.970
from other nightly
ETL jobs which

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:40.360
made them run slower, which
made them finish later,

00:04:40.360 --> 00:04:43.180
which ended up delaying all
of our business reporting

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.470
for that day.

00:04:45.470 --> 00:04:46.800
I was mortified.

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:49.340
I had not only made
my life harder,

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.550
I had made the database
administrator's life harder,

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:55.490
my team's life harder because
their stakeholders reporting

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:56.810
was delayed as well.

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.760
And needless to
say, the business

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.670
was not very happy with me.

00:05:01.670 --> 00:05:04.430
But lucky for me, the
DBA reached out to me

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:07.490
and offered to help
me rewrite this query.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:10.070
So I swallowed my
pride, walked down

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:13.430
to the 25th floor where
the engineering team sat,

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.410
pulled up a chair and
had a one hour meeting

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:20.067
that changed the course
of my SQL life forever.

00:05:20.067 --> 00:05:22.275
And what I learned I'm going
to share with you today.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:30.620
Imagine for a second that you're
going to explore a dark cave.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:33.145
What items might you
want to help you?

00:05:36.750 --> 00:05:37.270
For me,

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:38.520
I would want a map.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.980
I would want to know
where I'm going.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.090
Does this cave
even have an exit?

00:05:43.090 --> 00:05:46.630
How many tunnels are there?

00:05:46.630 --> 00:05:49.800
I would also want a
team or a cavern crew.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:52.600
I would want people to help
carry equipment and make

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:53.400
the journey easier.

00:05:56.060 --> 00:05:57.660
It's also very
dark in this cave.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:59.300
So a flashlight would
be helpful to be

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:00.467
able to see where I'm going.

00:06:05.220 --> 00:06:05.980
An Explain

00:06:05.980 --> 00:06:07.860
Plan is like a map.

00:06:07.860 --> 00:06:11.970
It returns a step by
step list of actions

00:06:11.970 --> 00:06:16.410
that your query will take
to return your data set.

00:06:16.410 --> 00:06:19.710
It also returns the
estimated computational cost

00:06:19.710 --> 00:06:22.740
of that query.

00:06:22.740 --> 00:06:24.130
It's very easy to run.

00:06:24.130 --> 00:06:27.990
You only have to put the word
explain in front of your query

00:06:27.990 --> 00:06:30.550
and run it.

00:06:30.550 --> 00:06:32.440
The first time I saw
an explain plan,

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:33.870
I was very overwhelmed.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:35.800
I swear my head exploded.

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.560
But it turns out that
it's a very helpful tool

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.560
because that
computational cost can

00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:43.930
help you understand if
changes that you're making

00:06:43.930 --> 00:06:46.330
to your query are helpful.

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:50.800
So in my instance, where I would
run my query, wait an hour,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:53.560
kill it, run it, wait
an hour, kill it.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:56.590
If I had run explain plans,
I could see that the changes

00:06:56.590 --> 00:06:59.620
that I would make,
that I was making

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.930
would reduce the
computational cost

00:07:01.930 --> 00:07:04.390
without having to
wait that extra hour.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:05.210
So explain

00:07:05.210 --> 00:07:14.280
plans are very useful. For
our cavern crew or cave team,

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.030
we want everyone to be
contributing equally.

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:20.520
We don't want one
person to be carrying

00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:22.590
all of the backpacks
and the other people

00:07:22.590 --> 00:07:24.210
to not be carrying anything.

00:07:24.210 --> 00:07:29.940
That doesn't seem, one very
nice, or two very efficient.

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.880
From a query perspective,
compute nodes

00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.580
are like your cave team.

00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:37.620
You want all of
your compute nodes

00:07:37.620 --> 00:07:40.890
contributing equally
and leveraging

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.240
all of their resources.

00:07:43.240 --> 00:07:46.690
To make sure that happens, you
need to appropriately specify

00:07:46.690 --> 00:07:47.775
the distribution style.

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.910
Distribution means that
your data is properly

00:07:52.910 --> 00:07:55.400
distributed across
the compute nodes

00:07:55.400 --> 00:07:58.225
and does not have
significant skew.

00:08:01.110 --> 00:08:02.470
But how do you do that?

00:08:02.470 --> 00:08:02.970
How do you know

00:08:02.970 --> 00:08:06.660
what's the right
distribution style?

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:09.695
You think about which column
you're going to join on.

00:08:13.510 --> 00:08:17.530
If you're planning on joining
on a unique ID, such as an order

00:08:17.530 --> 00:08:24.040
ID or a customer ID, then dist style
key is what you want to use.

00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:28.090
And the choice of your dist
key depends on which column

00:08:28.090 --> 00:08:33.320
you are going to use to
join to another data set.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:37.350
Dist style all is
very dangerous.

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:41.669
It copies your data across
each of the compute nodes.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:44.460
So in the case where
you're not going

00:08:44.460 --> 00:08:46.830
to join your table
to anything ever,

00:08:46.830 --> 00:08:48.960
and it's a small data
set, you can get away

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.280
with dist style all.

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:54.440
But I personally
wouldn't recommend it.

00:08:54.440 --> 00:08:56.810
And if you're not sure what
you're going to join on,

00:08:56.810 --> 00:08:59.930
maybe you have a table
that's a lot of rows,

00:08:59.930 --> 00:09:03.170
but it doesn't have
a good, unique ID

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:05.630
or a candidate for a distkey.

00:09:05.630 --> 00:09:07.670
And maybe it's a
combination of fields that's

00:09:07.670 --> 00:09:09.240
making it have a lot of rows.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:13.530
Then dist style even
is a good candidate.

00:09:13.530 --> 00:09:15.930
And the beauty of
the explain plan

00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:19.350
is that when we make
these changes to our table

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:22.110
and try to run our query,
we can run an explain

00:09:22.110 --> 00:09:25.420
plan to know if those changes
actually made a difference.

00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:32.605
So caution with dist style all.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.050
Your sort key is
like a flashlight.

00:09:40.050 --> 00:09:43.550
It helps you only
search relevant data.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:46.710
Imagine that you have a data set
that has three years of data,

00:09:46.710 --> 00:09:51.140
2020, 2021 and 2022.

00:09:51.140 --> 00:09:52.730
I only care about 2022.

00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:53.900
I'm living in the present.

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.480
So I have a where I have a
WHERE clause that filters

00:09:56.480 --> 00:10:00.600
on WHERE year equals 2022.

00:10:00.600 --> 00:10:03.630
And my results set
would be filtered.

00:10:03.630 --> 00:10:08.580
However, if I am not, if
the table I'm querying from

00:10:08.580 --> 00:10:10.530
does not have a sort key,

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:13.440
even though my results
set is filtered it,

00:10:13.440 --> 00:10:16.410
the query had to
scan the entire table

00:10:16.410 --> 00:10:21.180
to find it, which is
not very efficient.

00:10:21.180 --> 00:10:23.520
And so when you
specify a sort key,

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.162
it helps your query
know immediately

00:10:25.162 --> 00:10:27.120
where to find the data
that you're looking for.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.850
So it reduces the
computation needed

00:10:29.850 --> 00:10:31.250
to pull back your results set.

00:10:35.050 --> 00:10:36.530
To decide on a sort key,

00:10:36.530 --> 00:10:40.027
you think about which
column you will filter on.

00:10:40.027 --> 00:10:41.235
A lot of times that's a date.

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:47.420
This is an example
of the data set

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:49.610
that I was working with
on that fateful night when

00:10:49.610 --> 00:10:51.550
I ruined everything.

00:10:51.550 --> 00:10:57.820
There's a user ID, a session ID,
an event ID and an event time.

00:10:57.820 --> 00:11:01.180
There's also an event
type and a marketing tag.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.380
I was working on the
marketing analytics team.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:08.760
And so I cared about traffic
and sales driven by marketing.

00:11:08.760 --> 00:11:10.390
Based on what we just learned,

00:11:10.390 --> 00:11:12.510
we know that
distribution style key

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:14.550
is a good fit for
this table because it

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.890
has unique identifiers.

00:11:17.890 --> 00:11:20.835
But which unique identifier
would we choose as a dist key?

00:11:24.990 --> 00:11:28.040
The answer is it depends.

00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:30.800
If I was keeping the
data set at this level,

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:32.020
I would want to join.

00:11:32.020 --> 00:11:36.440
I would want to specify the
dist key as the most granular ID.

00:11:36.440 --> 00:11:39.680
I with using this data
set, I would likely

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.280
join on the event
ID to another table.

00:11:43.280 --> 00:11:45.490
So therefore it
should be the dist key.

00:11:49.250 --> 00:11:51.980
Pulling together everything
that we have learned.

00:11:51.980 --> 00:11:54.680
On the left hand
side, I have a query

00:11:54.680 --> 00:11:57.590
that takes that page view
level data that we were just

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:01.840
looking at and aggregates
it to the session level.

00:12:01.840 --> 00:12:03.670
If you can imagine
landing on a website,

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:06.180
you can have multiple page
views during a session.

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:09.580
And so I cared
about what marketing

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:11.830
is at the session level.

00:12:11.830 --> 00:12:15.130
And so when I'm creating
it, this temporary table,

00:12:15.130 --> 00:12:18.280
I've specified my
distribution style as key

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:21.490
and I've specified my
dist key as session ID

00:12:21.490 --> 00:12:23.350
because that's what
I'm going to use

00:12:23.350 --> 00:12:25.840
to join to another data set.

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:28.030
So I know that all
my compute nodes

00:12:28.030 --> 00:12:31.300
will be contributing equally.

00:12:31.300 --> 00:12:36.040
I also have my flashlight or my
sort key on session start time

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:39.640
because that's what I'm going
to use when I end up filtering

00:12:39.640 --> 00:12:40.920
from this temporary table.

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.840
On the right, I have a query
that leverages the table

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:52.430
that I just created and joins
it to another table called

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:54.360
web orders.

00:12:54.360 --> 00:12:57.845
And I'm joining to that table
on user ID and session ID.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:03.360
The because we have
specified session

00:13:03.360 --> 00:13:05.680
ID as a distribution key,

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:09.300
all of the compute nodes
will be used in this join

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:13.730
and it will make it run
much more efficiently.

00:13:13.730 --> 00:13:16.100
Because we have the sort
key specified as session

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:17.090
start time,

00:13:17.090 --> 00:13:20.780
I'm filtering on anything
greater than January 1st

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:24.650
of 2022, and so
therefore my query

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:27.650
doesn't have to search for data
that it doesn't care about.

00:13:27.650 --> 00:13:29.200
And we've saved
time there as well.

00:13:34.650 --> 00:13:38.370
We've run our query,
we have our results set

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.040
in a reasonable amount of time.

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:43.990
We've made it to the light
at the end of the tunnel.

00:13:43.990 --> 00:13:47.030
And the other side of the
cave, which is a big one.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:54.470
Some other wins that came
out of this experience.

00:13:54.470 --> 00:13:59.570
For one, my query that had
run overnight for seven hours

00:13:59.570 --> 00:14:01.700
ended up running
in under one hour

00:14:01.700 --> 00:14:03.990
during a high traffic
time on the database.

00:14:03.990 --> 00:14:07.490
So ultimately the runtime was
reduced by 6 hours because

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:09.940
of these changes.

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.100
I also built a
strong relationship

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:14.170
with the database
administrator that

00:14:14.170 --> 00:14:17.650
was very useful for future
cross-functional projects

00:14:17.650 --> 00:14:22.770
between the data science team
and the engineering team.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:29.150
But my favorite lesson came
from I documented my findings.

00:14:29.150 --> 00:14:32.530
And I shared it
back with my team.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:35.680
I helped them learn
these techniques

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:38.440
and apply them to
their own queries

00:14:38.440 --> 00:14:41.780
to avoid making the
mistakes that I made.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.390
And through that process
of sharing my experience

00:14:44.390 --> 00:14:48.290
with my team, it also
helped me have a paradigm

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:53.240
shift towards embracing
mistakes because mistakes,

00:14:53.240 --> 00:14:58.430
even very business
negative impacting

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:00.595
ones, can help you learn a lot.

00:15:04.040 --> 00:15:07.310
So if you find yourself
with a long running query,

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.990
there are a few
things you can do.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:12.750
You can run an explain plan.

00:15:12.750 --> 00:15:15.270
You can check the
distribution style

00:15:15.270 --> 00:15:18.060
and you can specify sort keys.

00:15:18.060 --> 00:15:20.880
And that is how you
can save hours of time

00:15:20.880 --> 00:15:23.610
with just a few lines of code.

00:15:23.610 --> 00:15:25.430
Thank you.

